   "html_text": "Eliot is explicitly critical of utilitarianism, particularly insofar as this approach depends on the calculation of consequences. One line of argument appeals to the nature of moral agents. Eliot suggests that moral motivation is largely emotional, and that the emotions are fundamentally unsuited to calculative thinking. As the narrator of “Janet’s Repentance” puts it, “emotion is obstinately irrational: it insists on caring for individuals; it absolutely refuses to adopt the quantitative view o",
